## Visualize and identify missing data patterns

### Missing values occurrence plot

-   We can use the `finalfit::missing_plot()` function:




    ::: {.cell}

    ```{.r .cell-code}
    p1 <- finalfit::missing_plot(
        pollutionlevels, 
        title = "pollutionlevels"
    )

    p2 <- finalfit::missing_plot(
        healthcheck, 
        title = "healthcheck"
    )

    p1 + p2
    ```

    ::: {.cell-output-display}
    ![](D02_analysis_missing_data_files/figure-html/finalfit_missing_plot-1.png){width=672}
    :::
    :::




    -   This plot shows the `row number` on the $x$-axis and the variables on the $y$-axis.

    -   It is used to get a quick overview of the missing values in each variable.

    -   The missing values are represented by the light blue bars.

    -   Variables with missing values are `noise` in `pollutionlevels`, and `rbc` in `healthcheck.`

-   Alternatively, we can use the `naniar::vis_miss()` function:




    ::: {.cell}

    ```{.r .cell-code}
    naniar::vis_miss(pollutionlevels)
    ```

    ::: {.cell-output-display}
    ![](D02_analysis_missing_data_files/figure-html/naniar_vis_miss-1.png){width=672}
    :::
    :::




    -   The plot shows the location of missing values as black bars.

    -   It also shows the percentage of missing values per variable (for the `noise` variable $50\%$) as well as the overall percentage of missingness $(12.5\%)$.

<br>

### Missing patterns plot

#### `pollutionlevels` dataset




::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
VIM::aggr(
    pollutionlevels,
    sortVars = TRUE,
    prop = FALSE,
    numbers = TRUE,
    ylab = c("N missings pollutionlevels", "Missing patterns \n pollutionlevels"),
)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](D02_analysis_missing_data_files/figure-html/aggr_pollutionlevels-1.png){width=672}
:::

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

 Variables sorted by number of missings: 
 Variable Count
    noise   100
    light     0
   visual     0
      air     0
```


:::
:::




-   The plot in the left panel is a bar plot showing the number (or proportion, by setting the argument `prop = TRUE`) of missing values in each variable. A table is also printed in the console showing the number (or proportion) of missing values in each variable.

-   The plot in the right panel shows the missing patterns in the dataset. There are two unique missing patterns:

    -   The first pattern has no missing values in any variable (i.e., all variables are observed and have light blue filling) and includes $100$ observations (cases).

    -   The second pattern has missing values in the `noise` variable only (red filling) and also includes $100$ observations (cases).

#### `healthcheck` dataset




::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
VIM::aggr(
    healthcheck,,
    sortVars = TRUE,
    prop = FALSE,
    numbers = TRUE,
    ylab = c("N missings healthcheck", "Missing patterns \n healthcheck"),
    cex.axis = 0.9
)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](D02_analysis_missing_data_files/figure-html/aggr_healthcheck-1.png){width=672}
:::

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

 Variables sorted by number of missings: 
  Variable Count
       rbc     8
 bone_mass     0
  body_fat     0
    height     0
       age     0
```


:::
:::




-   Similarly, There are two unique missing patterns in the `healthcheck` dataset:

    -   The first pattern has no missing values in any variable and includes $192$ observations (cases).

    -   The second pattern has missing values in the `rbc` variable only and includes $8$ observations (cases).

<!-- -->

-   The missing pattern can be also printed as a table using `finalfit::missing_pattern()`, `mice::md.pattern()`, or `misty::na.pattern()` functions:




    ::: {.cell}

    ```{.r .cell-code}
    misty::na.pattern(pollutionlevels)
    ```

    ::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

    ```
     Missing Data Pattern

      Pattern   n    perc light visual noise air nNA    pNA
            1 100  50.00%     1      1     1   1   0  0.00%
            2 100  50.00%     1      1     0   1   1 25.00%
              200 100.00%     0      0   100   0           
    ```


    :::
    :::




    -   The column `Pattern` shows the number of unique missingness patterns in the dataset.

    -   The column `n` shows the number of observations (cases) in each pattern.

    -   The column `perc` shows the percentage of observations in each pattern relative to the total number of observations in the dataset.

    -   The column `nNA` shows the number of variables with missing values in each pattern.

    -   The column `pNA` shows the perecentage of variables with missing values in each pattern relative to the total number of variables in the dataset.

    -   The other columns show the variables in the dataset and whether they are completely observed $(1)$ or possess missing values $(0)$.

    -   The rows show the unique missingnes patterns in the dataset, where each row represents a unique combination of observed and missing values for the variables.

    -   The last row shows the total number of observations $(200)$ and the number of missing values per variable.

    -   So, from the table we can see that there are two unique missingnes patterns:

        -   The first pattern has no missing values in any variable (i.e., all variables are observed). There are $100$ observations in this pattern, all variable columns have $1$ in this row, and the column `nNA` shows `0` as thereare no missing variables in this pattern.

        -   The second pattern has missing values in the `noise` variable only, so its column has $0$, while the other variables have `1` in this row. There are $100$ observations in this pattern, and the column `nNA` shows `1` as there is only one variable with missing values in this pattern.

### Missing values pairs plot:

-   This plot is used to assess the relationship between missingness and observed values of other variables (i.e., it examines how the distribution of observed variables differs between the missing and non-missing groups of variables that contain missing values).

#### `pollutionlevels` dataset




::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
missing_pairs(
  pollutionlevels,
  title = "Missing data matrix for pollutionlevels"
)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](D02_analysis_missing_data_files/figure-html/missing_pairs_pollutionlevels-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::




-   The differences in the distributions of `light`, `visual`, and `air` variables between the missing and non-missing groups of `noise` can be compared by looking at the third row. Column $3$ is the `noise` variable itself, so only the distribution of the non-missing group of `noise` is shown.

-   The distributions of `light`, `visual`, and `air` appear identical between the missing and non-missing groups of `noise`, suggesting that the missingness in `noise` is independent of these observed variables. This suggests that the missingness in `noise` is likely to be MCAR.

#### `healthcheck` dataset




::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
missing_pairs(
  healthcheck,
  title = "Missing data matrix for healthcheck"
)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](D02_analysis_missing_data_files/figure-html/missing_pairs_healthcheck-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::




-   The distribution of `age` shows substantial differences between the missing and non-missing groups of `rbc`. The missing group (grey box) of `rbc` has a smaller `age`, on average, compared to the non-missing group (light blue box).

-   This suggests that the missingness in `rbc` is related to the observed variable `age` and suggests that the missingness in `rbc` is likely to be MAR.

-   Alternatively, we can use `VIM::marginplot()` function to visualize a scatter plot between two variables at a time ans the distribution of the observed variable between the missing and non-missing values of variable with missing values in the plot margin:




    ::: {.cell}

    ```{.r .cell-code}
    VIM::marginplot(healthcheck[, c("rbc", "age")])
    ```

    ::: {.cell-output-display}
    ![](D02_analysis_missing_data_files/figure-html/margin_plot_healthcheck-1.png){width=672}
    :::
    :::




    -   The numbers in the bottom left corner of the plot represent the number of missing values in each variable ($8$ for `rbc` and $0$ for `age`) and the number of missing values in both variables ($0$).

    -   The red and blue boxes in the plot margin represent the distribution of `age` between the missing and non-missing groups of `rbc`, respectively. The distribution appears different (this is similar to what we have seen in the missing values pairs plot above).

### Assess the differences between missing and non-missing groups

-   The idea here is to create an `indicator variable` for each variable possessing missing values (considered here as the dependent variable).

-   In this `indicator variable` missing values are assigned $1$, while non-missing values are assigned $0$.

-   Then compare the distributions of the other variables (considered here as explanatory) between the two groups (i.e., missing and non-missing groups) using appropriate statistical tests (e.g., t-test or Chi-squared test):

    -   The assumptions of t-test and Chi-square test also apply to the comparisons mentioned above.

    -   These tests are only informative. There are some concerns pertaining to the number of simultaneous tests being conducted (up to $p(p-1)$ tests) and the correlation of the test statistics.

#### `pollutionlevels` dataset




::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Define the dependent variable (the variable with missing values)
dependent <- "noise"

# Define the explanatory variables (the completely observed variables)
explanatory <- setdiff(
  names(pollutionlevels),
  dependent
)

# Perform the comparison
missing_compare(
  pollutionlevels,
  dependent = dependent,
  explanatory = explanatory
) %>% gt()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}

